n about how the platform works so  we're not necessarily focused on coding  and knowing how to code and setting up  the environment we are focused on  demonstrating how the tools work and  then looking at projects that we can  analyze and gain experience from in the  application of these tools we're  combining theoretical knowledge with  analysis logic so we give an  introduction this course is going to be  focused on oncology and so the topics  the different projects that we're going  to be utilizing are all oncology related  but of course they have a difference in  the number of samples the background of  the samples the data itself might be a  little bit different here and there so  we're gonna take these different  variations of datasets and then we're  going to start by looking at suggested  pipelines pipelines that assemble  together a number of algorithms and that  are done in a sequential way processing  the raw data converting it into a visual  that we can interact with and finally  preparing and applying machine learning  algorithms on these data sets throughout  the preparation process you will be  seeing pop ups educational pop ups with  text explanations and links and those  will be included in the knowledge  assessment quizzes and finally an  evaluation on how you're able to apply  these different methods to different  types of data the course overview first  of all we started with this kickoff  workshop and we are discussing here  - practically approached the different  resources that we are providing  including courses the platform and the  project and then we are going to spend a  couple of weeks on introduction to  bioinformatics and online course that I  will show in a second and  transcriptomics one another online  course introduction to bioinformatics  covers big data challenges in biology  and biomedicine hypothesis free analysis  and biomedical applications of  bioinformatics and then transcriptomics  one will deal with next-generation  sequencing how to take raw  next-generation sequencing reads and  align them as well as quantify the  accumulated reads as a gene expression  level and explore that data using PCA  principal component analysis so that we  can visualize that data after these  basic courses are completed we are going  to come here to the Center for  computation and technology and have a  two-day workshop now this two-day  workshop is going to give you some  practical skills we will go through a  number of pipelines and compare results  and look at some of the methods that we  used in a pipeline and the second part  is going to be dedicated to an overview  of projects what types of projects we're  going to be dealing with and thinking  about different approaches that could be  relevant and interesting to individuals  and groups of participants within this  program after that workshop which is  going to happen on June 25 and 26 we are  going to spend a little more time going  through transcriptomics - and  transcriptomics 3 both of these courses  deal with quantification in a comparison  so the first option is differential gene  expression where you have a couple of  groups and you compare the different the  difference between gene expression  within samples own in those groups but  of course there are limitations to that  type of an approach for example  sometimes you have  a lot of variance within the group or  you might have more than two groups and  there's a challenge that could be  overcome with methods that now could be  described as machine learning methods or  data mining learning data mining methods  starting from unsupervised methods like  PCM clustering and in all the way to  building a supervised model for  classification like linear  discriminatory analysis support vector  machines and decision trees after that  we will have another hands-on workshop  and by that time we are hoping that  everybody has some level of project idea  that they can communicate and discuss in  a group 